[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-21", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 21 — the LAN column: 2047 ops/s at 8 slots, the SUT ceiling at ~3,500; and the road to No.1 stated", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-21.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 21 - the LAN column: from an idle host one switch away, the SUT's own ceiling is ~3,500 ops/s\n\n> Seed: iteration 20's S10 - the driver was a single nbb event loop on a\n> host at load 20-45 over a 10-14 ms rtt; a driver on the fleet LAN is a\n> different column and would show the SUT's ceiling. Judge:\n> bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung SF1 subset; origin :cross-host, path :lan.\n\n## Generate\n\n  levi (fleet mac-mini, load 1.6) -> simeon over 192.168.1.x, ICMP rtt\n  0.49/0.62/0.70 ms; nbb 1.3.200 via npx; the same driver file and the\n  same official parameters. HttpServer backlog raised to 1024 first\n  (128 slots had dropped connections in iteration 20).\n\n  Prediction: per-op p50 falls from 10-20 ms to low single digits; the\n  8-slot column moves from the wire to the SUT; the ceiling appears where\n  8 server threads run out on 10 cores.\n\n## Rank\n\n    slots  rate  on-time%  thru/s   ic02  ic07  ic08  ic09 (p50 ms)\n      8    2048  100.00   2047.3    3.4   2.2   1.8   5.9   <- vs 255.9 over Tailscale\n      8    3072   14.17   2259.2\n     32    3584  100.00   3452.6    8.7   7.4   7.1  12.9\n     32    4096   27.54   3466.0\n     64    3072  100.00   3070.9    4.8   2.8   2.3   7.9\n     64    4096   33.45   3510.3   17.8  16.6  16.2  21.8\n\n  Ratio to the audited No.1 (139,411 at SF300): 68 at 8 slots, 40 at 32.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Held. Same SUT, same data, same answers, 8x at 8 slots: the previous\n    columns measured the wire and the driver host. The benchmark's rule\n    that rows at different paths are different columns is what kept the\n    earlier rows honest rather than wrong.\n  - The SUT's ceiling is ~3,470-3,510 ops/s delivered, whichever the slot\n    count: ~2.3 ms of server time per operation on 8 threads. The engine\n    is under 0.5 ms of that for IC07/IC08 and ~3 ms for IC02/IC09 (the\n    drive's walk and independent half); the rest is HTTP, EDN parsing and\n    printing, and thread handoff. Ten cores, eight threads.\n\n## The road to No.1, stated at this row (asked by the owner today)\n\n  What this row is: 4 of 29 operations, a stated subset of SF1, one\n  10-core mac-mini, no updates, no short reads, our own driver, no\n  auditor. What the audited No.1 is: the full Interactive v1 workload\n  (14 complex reads, 7 short reads, 8 inserts with dependency tracking)\n  at SF100/300/1000 through the certified driver, ACID tests passed, an\n  independent auditor, one 160-core node. The two numbers do not share a\n  unit yet. In order, each gated by the previous:\n\n    M1  the whole workload on this rung: 29 operations behind the same\n        SUT, driven by the official ldbc_snb_interactive_driver through\n        a kotobase connector, validated against the driver's own\n        validation parameters. Until M1 no row is an LDBC number.\n    M2  updates: the 8 inserts interleaved with reads at the driver's\n        schedule, on a transacting SUT (today's SUT is a frozen hot db),\n        with the ACID test suite passing at the claimed isolation.\n    M3  SF30, the first audited rung: ~30x this data. The hot db costs\n        ~1.6 KB of heap per quad (1.3 GB for 810k), so SF30 does not\n        fit any host we have in memory; this is the persisted plane\n        (L0 packs, prolly indexes, an ordered on-disk value index) and\n        it is the largest step.\n    M4  SF100-SF300 and hardware: the No.1 ran 64 read threads on 160\n        cores; per core our 8 threads deliver ~440 ops/s/thread on a\n        trivial mix against their ~870/core on the full mix at SF300.\n    M5  the audit: LDBC membership, an auditor, the FDR, the ACID run.\n\n  What the loop can keep growing here: M1 (S3 short reads next, then the\n  remaining ICs), the engine's per-op cost, and the per-quad footprint\n  that gates M3. What it cannot: M4's hardware and M5's auditor are\n  decisions with a price, not iterations.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: BASELINE for the LAN path column\n(2047 at 8 slots, 3453 at 32). The Tailscale columns stand as their own\npath.\n\nSeeds for iteration 22, in order:\n\n  - S3 (mix, M1): short reads IS1-IS7 served and driven, with a reference\n    for each (Neo4j pairing in the harness), then IC01/IC03/... one at a\n    time. Every added operation is a new mix column.\n  - S11 (per-op cost): server threads 10 vs 8; EDN print of rows; the\n    drive's independent half as sets (S9).\n  - S2 (footprint, M3): bytes per quad on the hot db measured, then the\n    persisted plane's cold read path measured on the same queries.\n"}]
